<cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
<p><alert>(a)</alert></p>
<p>What is the average value of <m>f(x)</m> on <m>0\le x\le 2</m>?</p>
<p>avg value = <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><alert>(b)</alert></p>
<p>What is the average value of <m>g(x)</m> on <m>0\le x\le 2</m>?</p>
<p>avg value = <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><alert>(c)</alert></p>
<p>What is the average value of <m>f(x)\cdot g(x)</m> on <m>0\le x\le 2</m>?</p>
<p>avg value = <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p><alert>(d)</alert></p>
<p>Is the following statement true?
<me>\hbox{Average}(f)\cdot\hbox{Average(g)} = \hbox{Average}(f\cdot g)</me>
<var form="buttons">
<li>
<p>Yes</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>No</p>
</li>
</var></p>
</statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>\frac{1}{4}</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>\frac{3}{8}</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>0</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <solution><m>\int_0^2\,f(x)\,dx</m> and
<m>\int_0^2\,g(x)\,dx</m> as the area under the curves and dividing
by the length of the interval, which is 2.  This gives us:



<m>\hbox{Average}(f) = 0.25</m>, and



<m>\hbox{Average}(g) = 0.375</m>.



However, when we find <m>f(x)\cdot g(x)</m>, we find that the product
is everywhere zero!  <m>g(x)</m> is zero for <m>0\le x\le 1</m>, so
<m>f(x)\cdot g(x)</m> is zero there, and <m>g(x)</m> is zero for 
<m>1\le x\le 2</m>, so <m>f(x)\cdot g(x)</m> is zero there too.  Thus the
average value of the product is zero, and the average of the product
is not equal to the product of the averages!



</solution>
