      <solution><alert>(a)</alert>
<m>\int_{0}^{3}\,e^x\,dx = e^x\bigg|_{0}^{3} = e^3 - e^0 
  \approx 19.0855\ldots</m>




<alert>(b)</alert>
Computing the sums directly, since <m>\Delta x=1.5</m>, we have



LEFT(2) <m>= 1.5\cdot e^0 + 1.5\cdot e^{1.5} \approx 8.2225</m> 


RIGHT(2) <m>= 1.5\cdot e^{1.5} + 1.5\cdot e^{3} \approx 36.8509</m> 


TRAP(2) <m>= \frac 1 2 ( 8.2225 + 36.8509 ) \approx 22.5367</m> 


MID(2) <m>= 1.5\cdot e^{0.75} + 1.5\cdot e^{3\cdot 0.75} \approx 17.4072</m> 


and
SIMP(4) = (2 MID(2) + TRAP(2))/3 <m>\approx 19.117</m>.



And the errors are just the actual value minus the approximate values,
or



error(LEFT(2)) = 10.863; error(RIGHT(2)) = -17.7654;
error(TRAP(2)) = -3.4512; error(MID(2)) = 1.6784;
and error(SIMP(4)) = -0.0315.




<alert>(c)</alert>
Similarly, since <m>\Delta x = 0.375</m>, we have



LEFT(4) = 12.8149; error = 6.2707 


RIGHT(4) = 27.129; error = -8.0435 


TRAP(4) = 19.9719; error = -0.8864 


MID(4) = 18.6455; error = 0.44 


SIMP(8) = 19.0876; error = -0.0021.




<alert>(d)</alert>
For LEFT and RIGHT, we expect the error to go down by 1/2, and this
is very roughly what we see.  For MID and TRAP, we expect the error to
go down by 1/4, and this is approximately what we see.  For SIMP, we
expect the error to go down by 1/16, again as observed.



</solution>
